tel 1.0

base delicious
import soundness

info   the compiler's semantic diagnostics as trees, with their types reified and rendered as the user would write them
detail
    _Delicious_ reads the **semantic diagnostics** the proscala compiler emits under
    `-Zsemantic-diagnostics` (anthology's `scalacOptions.semanticDiagnostics`): compiler
    messages in which each type, symbol, name and code sample is marked up with
    private-use-character markers, and each type additionally carries its TASTy pickled
    form. A plain compiler message is text in which `List[Int]` is five characters; a
    semantic one knows that `List[Int]` is a type, which type, and that `List` is
    `scala.collection.immutable.List`.

    `Markup.parse` turns the marked-up text into a tree of `Markup` nodes — `Textual`,
    `Typed`, `Symbolic`, `Named`, `Code` and `Spanned` — and `SemanticMessage` wraps a
    whole message. A `Reifier`, given a classpath, unpickles each `Typed` node's TASTy
    payload back into a stenography `Syntax`, so the type can be rendered abbreviated
    against a set of imports, exactly as the user would write it; `render` does this for a
    whole message as text, and `teletype` as styled terminal output with every code sample
    and type syntax-highlighted by harlequin.

    ```scala fuel
    substitute ???  ...
    ##
    import soundness.*

    val notice: Notice = ???   // from a Scalac process run with scalacOptions.semanticDiagnostics
    val message: Optional[SemanticMessage] = notice.markup.let(SemanticMessage.parse(_))
    message.let(_.plain)         // the message with every marker removed
    message.let(_.types.size)    // how many types it mentions
    ```

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    ##
    val classpath: LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
    val reifier: Reifier = Reifier(classpath)
    given Imports = Imports.empty

    notice.markup.let(SemanticMessage.parse(_)).let(_.render(reifier))
    // Found: scala.collection.immutable.List[scala.Double]
    // Required: scala.collection.immutable.List[java.lang.String]
    ```

    Anything the compiler could not pickle — a local type, a skolem, a type variable, an
    error type — was replaced by a `Placeholder` before pickling, and is substituted back
    into the rendering by the name the compiler printed for it, so a diagnostic never
    becomes a crash: a payload which cannot be unpickled falls back to the compiler's own
    text. The library is split into three source sets:

    - `delicious.core` — `Markup`, `SemanticMessage`, `Placeholder`, `PlaceholderKind` and
      `Rendition`: pure parsing, depending on nothing but text, and available on every
      platform.
    - `delicious.scala` — `Reifier` and the `render` extension, which embed the Scala
      compiler to unpickle TASTy, and so are JVM-only.
    - `delicious.ansi` — the `teletype` extension, rendering a message with harlequin's
      syntax highlighting; JVM-only.
    - `delicious.compiler` — the `Shim` over the one compiler-internal constructor which
      differs between the Scala 3.9 and 3.10 streams; not for direct use.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella except `teletype`, which clashes
    there with escapade's `Teletypeable` rendering, and so is called as
    `delicious.teletype(message)(reifier)`.

refer delicious.SemanticMessage
refer delicious.Markup
refer delicious.Reifier
refer delicious.render

entry .SemanticMessage   a compiler message parsed into its markup tree
  detail
      A `SemanticMessage` is the `Markup` nodes of one marked-up compiler message, made by
      `SemanticMessage.parse` from the text an anthology `Notice#markup` carries when the
      compiler ran with `scalacOptions.semanticDiagnostics`. From it, `#plain` is the message
      with its markers stripped (the same text as the notice's own `message`), `#types` the
      `Markup.Typed` nodes it mentions, and `render` and `teletype` (with a `Reifier`) its
      renderings with every type reified.

      ```scala fuel
      substitute ???  ...
      ##
      import soundness.*

      val notice: Notice = ???
      notice.markup.let(SemanticMessage.parse(_)).let(_.types.map(_.plain))
      // the compiler-printed text of each type the message mentions
      ```
  refer delicious.Markup
  refer delicious.Reifier
  refer delicious.render
  entry .marked   whether a text contains semantic-diagnostic markers
      True when any character of the private-use range the markers use is present, so a
      message from a compiler without semantic diagnostics is cheaply told apart from one
      with them. The same test as `Markup.marked`.
  entry .parse   parses a marked-up message into a `SemanticMessage`
      `Markup.parse` applied to the whole text: a message with no markers parses to a single
      `Markup.Textual` node, so it is safe to call on any message.
  entry #markup   the message's nodes, in order
  entry #plain   the message with every marker removed, leaving only the visible text
  entry #types   every `Markup.Typed` node in the message, outermost first, nested ones included
      Found through `Named`, `Symbolic`, `Code` and `Spanned` nodes alike, and a `Typed`
      node's own `Typed` children follow it.

entry .Markup   one node of a semantic diagnostic: text, a type, a symbol, a name, code, or a span
  detail
      An enum of the node kinds a marked-up compiler message is made of, with `Markup.parse`
      to read a tree from the compiler's text and `Markup.plain` to strip it. A node is one
      of:

      ```tabular
      column  Case
      column  Node
      row  `Textual(text)`  plain text between markers
      row  `Typed(tasty, placeholders, rendition, children)`  a type: `tasty` is its TASTy pickled form as Base64 text (`Unset` when the compiler supplied none), reified by `Reifier#syntax`; `placeholders` stand in for the parts which could not be pickled; `children` are the compiler's printed rendering, the fallback
      row  `Symbolic(name, full, rendition, children)`  a symbol reference, by its `name` and `full`y qualified name
      row  `Named(isType, rendition, children)`  a name, with whether it is a type name
      row  `Code(rendition, children)`  a code sample, which `teletype` syntax-highlights
      row  `Spanned(kind, rendition, children)`  a marker of a `kind` this library does not know, kept with its children
      ```

      Every node but `Textual` has a `Rendition`, the form the compiler presented it in, and
      `children`, the nodes inside it; `#plain` on any node is its visible text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Markup.parse(t"type mismatch")   // List(Markup.Textual(t"type mismatch"))
      SemanticMessage.parse(t"Found: …").markup.map:
        case Markup.Typed(tasty, placeholders, _, children) => t"type"
        case Markup.Code(_, _)                              => t"code"
        case other                                          => other.plain
      ```
  refer delicious.SemanticMessage
  refer delicious.Placeholder
  refer delicious.Rendition
  entry .marked   whether a text contains any of the markers `parse` reads
      A character in the private-use range `U+E000` to `U+E003`.
  entry .parse   parses marked-up text into a list of nodes, as the compiler's own parser would
      Follows the compiler's `DiagnosticMarkup.parse` exactly: a marker opens with a start
      character, its kind, an attributes separator, `key=value` attributes (percent-encoded
      where a value holds a reserved character, decoded here) and a text separator, and
      closes with an end character. Anything which does not form a complete marker is
      dropped and the surrounding content kept as text — a stray start or end character
      vanishes, a truncated header reads as plain text — and the children of a marker never
      closed are spliced into its parent, so a message cut short still parses. The kind
      decides the node: `type`, `sym`, `name` and `code` have their own cases, and any
      other kind is `Spanned`.
  entry .plain   the text with every marker removed, keeping only the visible content
      Text with no markers is returned as it is, without parsing.
  entry #plain   this node's visible text: its own text, or its children's joined

entry .Placeholder   what stood in for a part of a type the compiler could not pickle
  detail
      The pickler which gives a `Markup.Typed` node its TASTy payload can only pickle what
      resolves against the classpath alone; a local type or term, a skolem, a type variable
      or an error type is replaced, as a whole maximal subtree, by a sentinel string-literal
      type `⟨scala-diag:N⟩` and recorded as a `Placeholder` numbered `N`, with its `#kind`,
      `#name`, `#arity`, where it was defined, and the text the compiler `#printed` for it.
      `Reifier.substitute` puts the printed text back where each sentinel stands, so
      `List[Bad.Local]` renders with `Bad.Local` even though `Local` is on no classpath.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Placeholder.decode(t"3|skolem|x|0|Test.scala:5|x.type")
      // Placeholder(3, PlaceholderKind.Skolem, t"x", 0, t"Test.scala:5", t"x.type")
      Placeholder.reference(t"⟨scala-diag:42⟩")          // 42
      Placeholder.escaped(t"⟨scala-diag:esc:real text⟩")  // t"real text"
      ```
  refer delicious.PlaceholderKind
  refer delicious.Reifier
  entry .Prefix   the reserved prefix of a placeholder sentinel, `⟨scala-diag:`
      A genuine string-literal type which happens to start with it is escaped by the
      compiler with the `esc:` form, which `escaped` reads back.
  entry .text   the sentinel text for a placeholder number: `⟨scala-diag:N⟩`
  entry .reference   the placeholder number, if a text is a sentinel reference
      `Unset` for any other text, including an escaped literal.
  entry .escaped   the original literal, if a text is an escaped genuine string literal
      `⟨scala-diag:esc:…⟩` gives back the `…`.
  entry .decode   a placeholder from its six `|`-separated fields, or `Unset`
      The fields are the number, the kind, the name, the arity, where it was defined (which
      may be empty) and the printed text, each percent-encoded where it would otherwise
      hold a `|`. A non-numeric number, or too few or too many fields, gives `Unset`;
      an unknown kind is kept as `PlaceholderKind.Other`.
  entry #id   the placeholder's number, as the sentinel refers to it
  entry #kind   what was replaced: a local type or term, an error, a type variable or a skolem
  entry #name   the name of what was replaced
  entry #arity   the number of type parameters of what was replaced
  entry #definedAt   where it was defined, as the compiler recorded it, or `Unset`
  entry #printed   the text the compiler printed for it, used in its place when rendering

entry .PlaceholderKind   the kind of thing a placeholder replaced
  detail
      `PlaceholderKind(text)` reads a kind from its wire name:

      ```tabular
      column  Case
      column  Wire name
      column  Replaced
      row  `LocalType`  `local-type`  a type definition local to the compilation
      row  `LocalTerm`  `local-term`  a term definition local to the compilation
      row  `Error`  `error`  an error type
      row  `Typevar`  `typevar`  an uninstantiated type variable
      row  `Skolem`  `skolem`  a skolem
      row  `Other(kind)`  anything else  a kind this library does not know, keeping the compiler's text
      ```
  entry .apply   a kind from its wire name, with an unknown name kept as `Other`

entry .Rendition   the form a symbol reference is presented in: default, declaration, located or full
  detail
      Carried by every `Markup` node but `Textual`. It is parsed from the marker's `style`
      attribute, but it is not visual styling; `Rendition(text)` reads it from its wire name:

      ```tabular
      column  Case
      column  Wire name
      column  The symbol is shown
      row  `Declaration`  `dcl`  as its declaration: `def act: Int`
      row  `Located`  `located`  as its located name
      row  `Full`  `full`  as its full name
      row  `Default`  anything else, or `Unset`  as it is normally printed
      ```
  entry .apply   a rendition from an optional wire name, `Default` when absent or unknown

entry .Reifier   unpickles the TASTy types in semantic diagnostics back into stenography `Syntax`
  detail
      A `Reifier(classpath)` holds a compiler context over a hellenism `LocalClasspath`,
      made lazily on first use, against which the TASTy payload of each `Markup.Typed` node
      is unpickled into a type and rendered as a stenography `Syntax`. The classpath should
      be the one the diagnosed code was compiled against — the pickler guarantees a payload
      resolves against the classpath alone, everything else having been replaced by a
      `Placeholder`, which `Reifier.substitute` puts back by its printed text.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      substitute ???  ...
      ##
      val classpath: LocalClasspath = LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]])
      val reifier: Reifier = Reifier(classpath)
      given Imports = Imports.empty

      val message: SemanticMessage = ???
      message.types.map(reifier.syntax(_).let(_.text))
      ```

      A reifier is a value to keep, not to make per message: its context loads the
      classpath's symbol table once. `render` and `teletype` take one, and `#imports` builds
      the `Imports` a rendering abbreviates against. In `delicious.scala`.
  refer delicious.render
  refer delicious.SemanticMessage
  refer delicious.Placeholder
  entry .substitute   rewrites the placeholder sentinels in a `Syntax` into their printed text
      Walks a `Syntax`, replacing each primitive whose text is a quoted `⟨scala-diag:N⟩`
      sentinel with `Syntax.Symbolic` of placeholder `N`'s printed text, and each escaped
      genuine literal `⟨scala-diag:esc:…⟩` with the literal it stood for. Sound because the
      pickler replaces whole maximal subtrees, so a sentinel is only ever a complete type,
      never a constructor or a prefix. Any other primitive is left alone.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val placeholder = Placeholder(0, PlaceholderKind.LocalType, t"Local", 0, Unset, t"Bad.Local")
      val list = Syntax.Simple(Designator(t"scala.collection.immutable.List"))
      val syntax = Syntax.Application(list, List(Syntax.Primitive(t"\"⟨scala-diag:0⟩\"")), false)
      Reifier.substitute(syntax, List(placeholder))
      // Syntax.Application(list, List(Syntax.Symbolic(t"Bad.Local")), false)
      ```
  entry #imports   an `Imports` for rendering against this classpath, with `export`s resolved
      `reifier.imports(designators, direct)` is `Imports.resolve` under the reifier's
      context: an `Imports` whose wildcard scopes are `designators` and whose directly
      imported names are `direct`, extended by the targets (type and companion) of every
      `export` alias declared in each of `designators`. This is what makes a type reached
      through a wildcard-imported prelude — `jacinta.Json` under `import soundness.*` —
      render by its leaf name, `Json`, as the user would write it, where an `Imports` built
      by hand renders it qualified. A scope which declares no exports, or which cannot be
      resolved, adds nothing and does not throw. Both sets are stdlib
      `scala.collection.immutable.Set[Designator]`s, as `Imports` itself holds them.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      val reifier: Reifier = Reifier(LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]]))
      val scope = scala.collection.immutable.Set(Designator(t"soundness"))
      given Imports = reifier.imports(scope, scala.collection.immutable.Set())
      Syntax.Simple(Designator(t"stenography#Syntax")).text   // t"Syntax"
      ```
  entry #syntax   the `Syntax` of a `Markup.Typed` node's payload, or `Unset` if it cannot be unpickled
      Decodes the node's Base64 TASTy, unpickles it as a type tree under the reifier's
      context, converts the type to a stenography `Syntax`, and applies
      `Reifier.substitute` with the node's placeholders. A node with no payload, or one
      whose payload cannot be unpickled (pickled by a different compiler version, or
      against another classpath), gives `Unset` rather than an error, so a caller falls
      back to the node's printed children.

entry .render   a semantic message as text, with every type re-rendered through stenography
    An extension on `SemanticMessage`, in `delicious.scala`:
    `message.render(reifier)(using Imports)` joins the message's nodes, rendering each
    `Markup.Typed` node as `Syntax#text` of `reifier.syntax(node)` — abbreviated according
    to the `Imports` in scope, so `List[Int]` reads as `List[Int]` under an import of
    `scala.*` and as `scala.collection.immutable.List[scala.Int]` under `Imports.empty` —
    and every other node as its visible text. A type which cannot be reified falls back to
    the compiler-printed text, so the result is never worse than `SemanticMessage#plain`.

    ```scala fuel
    alternative Plain
      code
          val message = SemanticMessage.parse(markedText)
          message.plain
          // Found: List[Double]  Required: List[String]
    alternative Fully qualified
      code
          val message = SemanticMessage.parse(markedText)
          val reifier = Reifier(LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]]))
          locally:
            given Imports = Imports.empty
            message.render(reifier)
            // Found: scala.collection.immutable.List[scala.Double]  Required: scala.collection.immutable.List[java.lang.String]
    alternative Abbreviated
      code
          val message = SemanticMessage.parse(markedText)
          val reifier = Reifier(LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]]))
          locally:
            given Imports = reifier.imports(scala.collection.immutable.Set(Designator(t"scala")), scala.collection.immutable.Set())
            message.render(reifier)
            // Found: List[Double]  Required: List[String]
    ##
    ```

entry .teletype   a semantic message as styled terminal text, with code and types syntax-highlighted
    An extension on `SemanticMessage`, in `delicious.ansi`, called as
    `delicious.teletype(message)(reifier)` — it is not exported to the umbrella, where
    `teletype` is escapade's `Teletypeable` rendering. It needs an `Imports`, a
    `ScalaSyntaxPalette` and a `Highlight` in scope (`Highlight.default`, tokenized, is
    found with no import). Each `Markup.Code` node is highlighted by harlequin as a Scala
    term, and each `Markup.Typed` node is reified through the `Reifier`, rendered against
    the `Imports`, and highlighted in type position; a type which cannot be reified is
    highlighted from the compiler's printed text instead. Any ANSI styling the compiler
    itself put in the text is stripped first, so the highlighting is from scratch.

    ```scala fuel
    typecheck
    substitute ???  ...
    ##
    given palette: ScalaSyntaxPalette = new Palette:
      type Form = Srgb
      def background: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Black
      def foreground: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.White
      def scalaError: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Red
      def scalaNumber: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.Gold
      def scalaString: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.LimeGreen
      def scalaTerm: Color in Srgb        = WebColors.Silver
      def scalaType: Color in Srgb        = WebColors.Turquoise
      def scalaKeyword: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.Orange
      def scalaSymbol: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.Gray
      def scalaParenthesis: Color in Srgb = WebColors.Gray
      def scalaModifier: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.Orchid
      def scalaComment: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.DimGray
      def subdued: Color in Srgb          = WebColors.DimGray
      def accented: Color in Srgb         = WebColors.White
      def margin: Color in Srgb           = WebColors.DimGray

    val reifier = Reifier(LocalClasspath.of(Classloader[Option[Int]]))
    given Imports = Imports.empty
    val message: SemanticMessage = ???

    val styled: Teletype = delicious.teletype(message)(reifier)
    styled.plain   // the same text as message.render(reifier)
    ```

    No library ships a `ScalaSyntaxPalette`, since a palette is a choice of colours: a
    program defines one as a given, as the sample does, with a colour for each accent.

entry .Shim   the spelling of the compiler-internal API delicious wraps, per compiler stream
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. `delicious.compiler` holds one `Shim` for the Scala 3.9 stream and
      one for 3.10, defining the same name against each stream's shapes, so that nothing
      else in delicious knows which is in play.
  entry .compilationUnitInfo   a `CompilationUnitInfo` over a virtual file and its `TastyInfo`
      3.9 takes the `TastyInfo` by value and 3.10 as a lazy loader; the shim supplies it as
      each stream expects, always present, since the tree unpickler reads it
      unconditionally.
